  "recipe": {
    "technique": "Hebron glass was traditionally produced using sand from the village of Bani Na'im, east of Hebron, and soda-rich sodium carbonate plant-ash from salt-resistant (halophytic) plants around the Dead Sea, approximately 35 miles away (Seetzen, 1859: 49). Instead of sand, recycled glass is the primary raw material used to make Hebron glass today. In the 19th hundred, it was reported that Arabs cut and burned Salicornia fruticosa (syn. Arthrocnemium fruticosum), growing by the Dead Sea, and afterwards sold the ashes to the glass-makes of Hebron."
  },
